#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128938 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28a9233c-6ff6-5ed0-b59b-e7f9807b4ffd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Spherical Coordinates of a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "8cca7ba8-24dc-5968-99a4-8d08592954fc" :question/math-academy-id "e-6225" :question/problem "What are the spherical polar coordinates of the point $P?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/assets/02219461392f94791f20e3e7f0e125e846b602862524b381fa909530e0f4e9d6.png)" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho,\\theta,\\phi),$ where $\\rho \\geq 0,$ $0 \\leq \\theta <   2\\pi,$ and $0 \\leq \\phi \\leq \\pi.$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following: \n\n- The distance from the origin to the point $P$ equals $4$ units.\n\n- The half-plane that contains the point $P$ and whose boundary is the $z$-axis forms an angle of \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}2-\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{10}\n$$\n\n radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and the point $P$ forms an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ radians with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $P$ are $\\left(4, \\dfrac{3\\pi}{10}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\right).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128939 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbfe5ff1-14d6-5e0f-87be-3c9552f7868f" :knowledge-point/title "Converting the Spherical Coordinates of a Point Into the Cartesian Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "7007e89d-fe92-5cc9-a0d1-49f34036176f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6226" :question/problem "The point $Q$ has spherical coordinates $\\left(2\\sqrt3, \\pi, \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6} \\right).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $Q.$" :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad\ny = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad \nz = \\rho\\cos\\phi.\n$$\n\nCalculating $x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $Q$ are $(-\\sqrt3, 0, -3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128940 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "807f1cec-5e90-50be-afd1-0bf627c50fb0" :knowledge-point/title "Converting the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Into the Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "cadaa861-db49-58bc-97a5-42c3dda4fa75" :question/math-academy-id "e-6227" :question/problem "The point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-\\sqrt2,0,\\sqrt2).$ What are the spherical polar coordinates of  $P?$" :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad \ny = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad \nz = \\rho\\cos\\phi.\n$$\n\nFrom the above equations, we can express $\\rho$, $\\theta$, and $\\phi$ in terms of $x$, $y$, and $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\rho = \\sqrt{x^2+y^2+z^2}, \\qquad\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{y}{x}, \\qquad\n\\cos\\phi = \\dfrac{z}{\\rho}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\rho\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{2})^{2}+{0}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2+0+2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $(x,y) = (-\\sqrt2,0)$ lies on the negative $x$-axis, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, for $\\phi,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{z}{\\rho }) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $P$ are $\\left(2, \\pi,  \\dfrac{\\pi}4 \\right).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297878 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbfe5ff1-14d6-5e0f-87be-3c9552f7868f" :knowledge-point/title "Converting the Spherical Coordinates of a Point Into the Cartesian Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ea84e8a-6615-5b37-bbb0-41c46a77c690" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},-\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{3}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297880} {:answer/id #uuid "3e6607f5-0190-5b4b-a365-040cd221d307" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{4},-\\frac{3}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297881} {:answer/id #uuid "5c532576-052c-54d6-894f-a8b076a54904" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},-\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297882} {:answer/id #uuid "20ac82b6-7e80-5bdd-82fc-ac2763b575f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},-\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297883} {:answer/id #uuid "f7c182ad-48b0-5a5a-9373-6746255eb836" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},-\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c532576-052c-54d6-894f-a8b076a54904" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},-\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297882} :answer-field/id #uuid "47cdb975-c902-50e4-a23b-e2e40accd173" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "613c94d2-7fac-5e2b-b2b7-18ae60e90291" :question/math-academy-id "q-165297" :question/problem "The point $H$ has spherical polar coordinates $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{4\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $H.$" :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $z$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $H$ are $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},-\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{3}{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297885 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "807f1cec-5e90-50be-afd1-0bf627c50fb0" :knowledge-point/title "Converting the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Into the Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82954505-9375-5455-87de-2f1bcaa17553" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{7\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297887} {:answer/id #uuid "02fa50ce-38a5-56a2-b9af-540c9d648403" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{11\\pi }{6},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297888} {:answer/id #uuid "9018945d-bc83-5c68-a522-4065274ba2ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{7\\pi }{6},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297889} {:answer/id #uuid "44055913-f6fd-5979-9b14-81f1813a4e69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297890} {:answer/id #uuid "2c49a66e-3402-5cc4-9fbf-97fb98c40cd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{11\\pi }{6},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9018945d-bc83-5c68-a522-4065274ba2ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{7\\pi }{6},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297889} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fde7d56-8839-5098-8c70-ac6f48c3ae9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297886}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5090116f-465b-5350-9f1c-5fb94addf706" :question/math-academy-id "q-165299" :question/problem "The point $K$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2},-1).$ What are the spherical polar coordinates of $K?$" :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nFrom the above equations, we can express $\\rho $, $\\theta $, and $ϕ$ in terms of $x$, $y$, and $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{y}{x},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\frac{z}{\\rho }\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\rho :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $(x,y)=(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2})$  lies in the third quadrant. So to find $\\theta ,$ we first need to find the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-\\frac{1}{2}}{-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we add $\\pi $ to the reference angle to get $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & ={\\theta }_{R}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $ϕ$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{z}{\\rho }) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $K$ are $(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{7\\pi }{6},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297892 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28a9233c-6ff6-5ed0-b59b-e7f9807b4ffd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Spherical Coordinates of a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1917214-948c-5b69-9881-d8f246ddcc8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{8},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297894} {:answer/id #uuid "8950879d-635c-5419-836c-41d5d73fb7fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{8},\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297895} {:answer/id #uuid "8223831b-ae0e-5e7a-ae30-282cd18687f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{8},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297896} {:answer/id #uuid "d83ea85a-4b05-5f29-9f19-35bd3aed3278" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{7\\pi }{8},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297897} {:answer/id #uuid "ab729ad4-5e99-5933-8bfe-d4f3a5d01265" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{8},\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297898}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1917214-948c-5b69-9881-d8f246ddcc8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{8},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297894} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e906ad8-cabf-582b-bdd1-66c1fd6847db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297893}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0af17017-1beb-5267-afad-9e99f273fd62" :question/math-academy-id "q-165301" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/assets/0f89beaaf82692e398a3259140b735feb925395c6e6d9f63b72a5406d0d5073f.png)\n\nWhat are the spherical polar coordinates of the point $Q?$" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where $\\rho \\ge 0,$ $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ and $0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi .$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n- The distance from the origin to the point $Q$ equals $\\sqrt{2}$ units.\n\n- The half-plane that contains the point $Q$ and whose boundary is the $z$-axis forms an angle of \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{8}=\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\n$$\n\n radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and the point $Q$ forms an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ radians with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $Q$ are $(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{8},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297899 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28a9233c-6ff6-5ed0-b59b-e7f9807b4ffd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Spherical Coordinates of a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c539892-c439-51c3-ba3b-a9866fa4eb72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,\\frac{\\pi }{7},\\frac{2\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297901} {:answer/id #uuid "68da712d-6c9d-5288-9462-ca63ecc49a65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,\\frac{6\\pi }{7},\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297902} {:answer/id #uuid "c2eb3481-b975-56bc-8d9e-5aa08117f2a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,\\frac{5\\pi }{14},\\frac{2\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297903} {:answer/id #uuid "a1e63fdd-7089-53b3-8a75-02fcb4b9b1bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,\\frac{\\pi }{14},\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297904} {:answer/id #uuid "c11300fc-dd1f-5a3d-8985-488f97acf48c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,\\frac{5\\pi }{14},\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297905}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2eb3481-b975-56bc-8d9e-5aa08117f2a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,\\frac{5\\pi }{14},\\frac{2\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297903} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab4a73ed-e9cf-5601-89e1-0475f00549a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cae192ae-df7b-5736-8fcd-5ea3c5be38e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-165340" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/assets/57ce4048bd9357072fadcee7fb65c5e9c2823572ec48b8bb04fbaad3c464aa3d.png)\n\nWhat are the spherical polar coordinates of the point $Q?$" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where $\\rho \\ge 0,$ $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ and $0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi .$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n- The distance from the origin to the point $Q$ equals $3$ units.\n\n- The half-plane that contains the point $Q$ and whose boundary is the $z$-axis forms an angle of \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{5\\pi }{14}\n$$\n\n radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and the point $Q$ forms an angle of \n\n$$\n\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\n radians with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $Q$ are $(3,\\frac{5\\pi }{14},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129800 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28a9233c-6ff6-5ed0-b59b-e7f9807b4ffd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Spherical Coordinates of a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90ce1e80-3187-5483-b3d9-f92b8a8a18af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-\\frac{π}{3},-\\frac{π}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128595} {:answer/id #uuid "968dde5b-b165-5a3c-add4-b373ea2158b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2, \\frac{π}{3},-\\frac{π}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128596} {:answer/id #uuid "edc7e67c-f93d-51fc-a7f4-9ba66bc16b3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2, \\frac{π}{3}, \\frac{π}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128597} {:answer/id #uuid "19008d38-8a34-501d-a63d-9b1773792dbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2, \\frac{π}{4}, \\frac{π}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128598} {:answer/id #uuid "2a2789ea-9587-5da3-bd77-8052bfcbc6cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2, \\frac{π}{2}, \\frac{π}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "edc7e67c-f93d-51fc-a7f4-9ba66bc16b3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2, \\frac{π}{3}, \\frac{π}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128597} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ba7f1f8-ab48-52dc-97d4-e15b17d6f6d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128594}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6ff8d6ca-d0bb-5b1c-8a89-3e8d60a00c7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-74320" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/assets/a9f9418d0a9984af019254cb06d9b2aab3251dd3127f05b89b118620ac6dd7e2.png)\n\nWhat are the spherical polar coordinates of the point $A?$" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where $\\rho \\ge 0,$ $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ and $0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi .$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n- The distance from the origin to the point $A$ equals $2$ units.\n\n- The half-plane that contains the point $A$ and whose boundary is the $z$-axis forms an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and the point $A$ forms an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ radians with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $A$ are $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129803 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "807f1cec-5e90-50be-afd1-0bf627c50fb0" :knowledge-point/title "Converting the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Into the Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2b40554-7869-5477-867c-7ff9b6973661" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2, \\frac{7π}{4},-\\frac{π}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128613} {:answer/id #uuid "dbbd063f-f9e9-5188-9f6c-e73d644c9346" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2}, \\frac{π}{4}, \\frac{π}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128614} {:answer/id #uuid "1601d3e0-e375-514e-bf17-b5eb052d3add" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-\\frac{7π}{4}, \\frac{π}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128615} {:answer/id #uuid "4b444044-8381-50f3-8469-fd9a6888b096" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2, \\frac{7π}{4}, \\frac{π}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128616} {:answer/id #uuid "78ab90ea-c7b6-5dd2-8c88-e7e062c633d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2}, \\frac{3π}{4}, \\frac{π}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b444044-8381-50f3-8469-fd9a6888b096" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2, \\frac{7π}{4}, \\frac{π}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128616} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb89162c-b6de-583b-98d4-1a11b7b9fcbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128612}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f8ecc8a0-b267-5e1b-b503-03f75182ab5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-74341" :question/problem "The point $A$ has Cartesian coordinates $(1,-1,\\sqrt{2}).$ What are the spherical polar coordinates of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nFrom the above equations, we can express $\\rho $, $\\theta $, and $ϕ$ in terms of $x$, $y$, and $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{y}{x},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\frac{z}{\\rho }\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\rho :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1+2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $(x,y)=(1,-1)$ lies in the fourth quadrant. So to find $\\theta ,$ we first need to find the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-1}{1}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract the reference angle from $2\\pi $ to get $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $ϕ$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{z}{\\rho }) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $A$ are $(2,\\frac{7\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129804 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "807f1cec-5e90-50be-afd1-0bf627c50fb0" :knowledge-point/title "Converting the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Into the Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1984aa8-cfbe-5de2-9bf9-856fc0a5a079" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2}, 2.034, 2.230)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128619} {:answer/id #uuid "20c675a1-d058-5901-83bc-cd4f349e5923" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2}, 2.034, 1.251)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128620} {:answer/id #uuid "3749dab4-ae47-5c21-a140-a9a8eb3a1407" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2}, 1.251, 2.457)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128621} {:answer/id #uuid "388fe4ba-f0eb-5b47-8d84-4f4b65a4b7c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2}, 2.034, 0.769)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128622} {:answer/id #uuid "4457ba34-1293-5b48-9d1a-6758d17412ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2}, 0.769, 2.034)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128623}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1984aa8-cfbe-5de2-9bf9-856fc0a5a079" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2}, 2.034, 2.230)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128619} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3acd0bf-4dd6-5f13-a8ea-2680b6936179" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128618}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e01b0b14-fb55-55cd-afc5-3b1a6375987e" :question/math-academy-id "q-74342" :question/problem "The point $B$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-1,2,-\\sqrt{3}).$ What are the spherical polar coordinates $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ of $B?$ Express $\\theta $ and $ϕ$ in radians rounded to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nFrom the above equations, we can express $\\rho $, $\\theta $, and $ϕ$ in terms of $x$, $y$, and $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{y}{x},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\frac{z}{\\rho }\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\rho :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4+3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $(x,y)=(-1,2)$ lies in the second quadrant. So to find $\\theta $, we first need to find the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{2}{-1}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2) \\\\  & ≈1.107\\,149\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract the reference angle from $\\pi $ to get $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ≈\\pi -1.107\\,149 \\\\  & ≈2.034\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $ϕ$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{z}{\\rho }) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{-\\sqrt{3}}{2\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{8}}) \\\\  & ≈2.230.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $B$ are $(\\sqrt{5},2.034,2.230).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297906 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28a9233c-6ff6-5ed0-b59b-e7f9807b4ffd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Spherical Coordinates of a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9105c00-8f26-58ef-9314-1c43ae6856c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297908} {:answer/id #uuid "5d2e0407-14e8-5c3e-9cd9-380108060fd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297909} {:answer/id #uuid "a19f9326-fd2c-5d03-b1c2-9e761f764655" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4},-\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297910} {:answer/id #uuid "181de3a2-c42a-5a46-b5f7-aeb53e217258" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297911} {:answer/id #uuid "167dfa94-8254-516f-9029-c952b8f60b5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297912}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d2e0407-14e8-5c3e-9cd9-380108060fd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297909} :answer-field/id #uuid "526db7c3-53a9-5944-80ca-d23fabddfc7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297907}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "85f63fbc-f496-529a-be03-0b80cffd8bc3" :question/math-academy-id "q-74542" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/assets/fe4ff907283c9fbce1907651c4047625d750eddfc75f9a9c34f845c3a92b6036.png)\n\nWhat are the spherical polar coordinates of the point $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that we write the position of a point in spherical coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where $\\rho \\ge 0,$ $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ and $0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi .$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n- The distance from the origin to the point $P$ equals $4$ units.\n\n- The half-plane that contains the point $P$ and whose boundary is the $z$-axis forms an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and the point $P$ forms an angle of \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\n radians with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $P$ are $(4,\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129806 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28a9233c-6ff6-5ed0-b59b-e7f9807b4ffd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Spherical Coordinates of a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca56d43d-8eff-5318-b862-44a92fdb5daf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297914} {:answer/id #uuid "2e7e1cf0-2c52-536d-a31c-17254ddbbb9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297915} {:answer/id #uuid "5819308e-eb0a-54d8-8a30-9d114387016a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297916} {:answer/id #uuid "3d0ded1a-1153-5a56-a287-eb66fc9c4f9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{-\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297917} {:answer/id #uuid "0331f0bb-f91b-5435-8853-2f17a2608d72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297918}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e7e1cf0-2c52-536d-a31c-17254ddbbb9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297915} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef6826b0-229d-5d69-a68c-dae16b115aea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297913}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4cb17daf-65cc-5356-9bc7-0f455d0d0de3" :question/math-academy-id "q-74543" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/assets/561430ec00f405b6cce3ab743de1665fce8909f0a505bcd0df05478badafae35.png)\n\nWhat are the spherical polar coordinates of the point $D?$" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where $\\rho \\ge 0,$ $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ and $0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi .$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n- The distance from the origin to the point $D$ equals $2$ units.\n\n- The half-plane that contains the point $D$ and whose boundary is the $z$-axis forms an angle of \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\n radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and the point $D$ forms an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ radians with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $D$ are $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297919 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbfe5ff1-14d6-5e0f-87be-3c9552f7868f" :knowledge-point/title "Converting the Spherical Coordinates of a Point Into the Cartesian Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe465518-af88-50fb-a0ac-da057861cec1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297921} {:answer/id #uuid "cd1e01dd-ed33-58d5-8aa8-2af50db07734" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297922} {:answer/id #uuid "e20963b8-ca2c-564e-8523-3bfef480c98f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,0,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297923} {:answer/id #uuid "6d55643c-58be-544f-ac93-eb3885a48693" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-1,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297924} {:answer/id #uuid "8b846d6f-b6fc-5c11-96c6-6d73034cca97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297925}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e20963b8-ca2c-564e-8523-3bfef480c98f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,0,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297923} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b24941b-76b7-5d9f-8639-180d0ae1b4fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297920}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e5768a91-c888-538c-93f2-c44301985e0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-74550" :question/problem "The point $D$ has spherical coordinates $(\\sqrt{2},\\pi ,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $D.$" :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 0\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $z$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $D$ are $(-1,0,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297926 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbfe5ff1-14d6-5e0f-87be-3c9552f7868f" :knowledge-point/title "Converting the Spherical Coordinates of a Point Into the Cartesian Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "246e72be-cc71-592a-874c-e8cdba68e0a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{3},0,-\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297928} {:answer/id #uuid "96f0196d-eab9-5e71-b02b-479d4c267768" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{3},3,\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297929} {:answer/id #uuid "35190b1e-6c66-50d8-8706-647b767b38df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\sqrt{3},\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297930} {:answer/id #uuid "42f81c98-1976-5050-9629-8560de47f75a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{3},0,\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297931} {:answer/id #uuid "ab5a388f-43c9-5ccc-9085-980c0e564a9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\sqrt{3},3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297932}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab5a388f-43c9-5ccc-9085-980c0e564a9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\sqrt{3},3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297932} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dce7c85-0015-5e23-b3ca-75b6cf3883c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297927}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ea4a5d5e-f7f7-56bd-991c-22b5db64ad0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-74551" :question/problem "The point $B$ has spherical coordinates $(2\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B.$" :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $z$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(0,\\sqrt{3},3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297933 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbfe5ff1-14d6-5e0f-87be-3c9552f7868f" :knowledge-point/title "Converting the Spherical Coordinates of a Point Into the Cartesian Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aef57349-e792-578f-ad87-fbd8a2cc52c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\sqrt{3},\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297935} {:answer/id #uuid "91cd9b22-18b6-5f66-8081-913606713657" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,\\sqrt{3},\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297936} {:answer/id #uuid "0974dc6f-258c-5a31-bccb-3b32cd631aa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\sqrt{3},2\\sqrt{3},\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297937} {:answer/id #uuid "bfac0cf0-114c-56e3-834b-2470a1644fd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\sqrt{3},-2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297938} {:answer/id #uuid "9e3599f7-91a5-5aaa-9968-500c1d40e96b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-2\\sqrt{3},\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfac0cf0-114c-56e3-834b-2470a1644fd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\sqrt{3},-2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297938} :answer-field/id #uuid "0edf1b1a-bc7c-541a-948c-8cbb1a45fc58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d0050754-0064-5d7f-9d52-a3eaae555e94" :question/math-academy-id "q-74554" :question/problem "The point $H$ has spherical polar coordinates $(4,\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $H.$" :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $z$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $H$ are $(1,\\sqrt{3},-2\\sqrt{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297940 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbfe5ff1-14d6-5e0f-87be-3c9552f7868f" :knowledge-point/title "Converting the Spherical Coordinates of a Point Into the Cartesian Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df35ee61-64b2-5a87-8156-5b1f393b2033" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\sqrt{3},-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297942} {:answer/id #uuid "7605f1ac-6fc2-5ac4-a345-b53f0596afd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,\\sqrt{3},-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297943} {:answer/id #uuid "def9c837-70e9-5f5a-bcc9-275303f4b170" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,2\\sqrt{3},-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297944} {:answer/id #uuid "7175b52a-b645-541c-8141-b4b3e37f815e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\sqrt{3},-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297945} {:answer/id #uuid "abb65a18-8f76-5e5f-89bb-1f51f8391a82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,2\\sqrt{3},-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297946}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "def9c837-70e9-5f5a-bcc9-275303f4b170" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,2\\sqrt{3},-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297944} :answer-field/id #uuid "b43aa9b6-add9-531f-8418-e0fa1d7bd699" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297941}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9c504b49-7f27-5f56-866c-26d1ad54db4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-74555" :question/problem "The point $P$ has spherical polar coordinates $(4\\sqrt{2},\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$" :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $z$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-2,2\\sqrt{3},-4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297947 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "807f1cec-5e90-50be-afd1-0bf627c50fb0" :knowledge-point/title "Converting the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Into the Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98fb0622-7b79-57cc-bc99-813ef3db9e0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297949} {:answer/id #uuid "7d063f2b-ab57-5010-9c83-922134102066" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297950} {:answer/id #uuid "19b8583f-80c2-505e-946e-8f2d76d88ffb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297951} {:answer/id #uuid "67850a41-5022-547f-9055-c4e390ab1894" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{3},\\frac{7\\pi }{6},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297952} {:answer/id #uuid "e65a2972-0cac-5158-9c0a-4c5718670fe4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98fb0622-7b79-57cc-bc99-813ef3db9e0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297949} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c6ab954-54de-59c2-8f1e-9029c3b4599d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297948}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "df22d970-821c-535e-a8ba-c3e0c193b6b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-74720" :question/problem "The point $U$ has Cartesian coordinates $(1,-\\sqrt{3},2).$ What are the spherical polar coordinates of $U?$" :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nFrom the above equations, we can express $\\rho $, $\\theta $, and $ϕ$ in terms of $x$, $y$, and $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{y}{x},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\frac{z}{\\rho }\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\rho :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+3+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $(x,y)=(1,-\\sqrt{3})$ lies in the fourth quadrant. So to find $\\theta ,$ we first need to find the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-\\sqrt{3}}{1}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract the reference angle from $2\\pi $ to get $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $ϕ$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{z}{\\rho }) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{2}{2\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $U$ are $(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297954 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "807f1cec-5e90-50be-afd1-0bf627c50fb0" :knowledge-point/title "Converting the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Into the Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90fa3a93-542f-5870-afac-f679c0175873" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0.991,6,0.975)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297956} {:answer/id #uuid "1d3b1ceb-33e9-5c43-a2fe-f9f83393deaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,0.991,0.957)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297957} {:answer/id #uuid "57052a14-d506-5281-ad93-d0b4607ce817" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(10,0.199,0.795)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297958} {:answer/id #uuid "7151d119-50ac-5e2e-8870-d010546ce97f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{10},0.199,0.759)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297959} {:answer/id #uuid "9def0302-64ad-5360-b62d-2c6b71b2fe39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,0.919,0.975)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57052a14-d506-5281-ad93-d0b4607ce817" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(10,0.199,0.795)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297958} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ac891ce-cd46-5f61-8a17-d015d223cb0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3cbb4f71-8dff-56c2-b60d-738177511ef4" :question/math-academy-id "q-74725" :question/problem "The point $S$ has Cartesian coordinates $(7,\\sqrt{2},7).$ What are the spherical polar coordinates $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ of $S?$ Express $\\theta $ and $ϕ$ in radians rounded to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nFrom the above equations, we can express $\\rho $, $\\theta $, and $ϕ$ in terms of $x$, $y$, and $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{y}{x},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\frac{z}{\\rho }\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\rho :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{7}^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+{7}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{49+2+49} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100} \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $(x,y)=(7,\\sqrt{2})$ lies in the first quadrant,  we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{7}) \\\\  & ≈0.199\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $ϕ$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{z}{\\rho }) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{7}{10}) \\\\  & ≈0.795.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $S$ are $(10,0.199,0.795).$"}]}
